 3. Exclusive: Using OpenAI&#x27;s new image editor 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Various AI-generated images of Ina's cat Raven using ChatGPT Images 2.5. Screenshots: Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 OpenAI launched ChatGPT Images 2.5 yesterday, the company shared first with Axios.

 Why it matters: For all their capabilities, AI image generators have struggled in the past to keep people and pets looking like themselves, and editing tools have been limited.

 The big picture: The updated image engine within ChatGPT is not only faster than its predecessor, but does a much better job preserving the likeness of people and pets and allows for more detailed editing, according to Axios' early testing of the model.

 Between the lines: Any discussion of AI image generation is incomplete without acknowledging its ethical implications.

 I've been an enthusiastic early user, making soccer cards for my son's team, turning photos into coloring pages for parties, and designing logos. But I've also heard strong pushback from friends who now find themselves competing with systems built, in part, on uncompensated creative labor. The other side: "The sketch tool and these templates are kind of an attempt to make people more engaged in the process of creation, rather than them being a passive force," Adele Li, OpenAI's product lead on images, told Axios.

 "I don't want to be able to see ChatGPT in the world," she said. "I want people to be able to generate and express their own individualism." Keep reading. 

 
 
